  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 251 AM CDT Fri Sep 11 2026\n\nClear skies are present across the Upper Midwest early this\nmorning. Clouds will increase today as a disturbance over\nnorthern WY currently ejects eastward to the northern Plains \nand Upper Midwest by this evening. Models indicate this wave \nwill weaken with time with the best forcing remaining across \nnorthern MN. However, a LLJ will strengthen through the day to \n45-50 kts overhead/across the warm sector and a narrow plume of\nhigher pwats (~1.5 inches) will be present just ahead of a \nsurface cold front. That, in combination with fairly steep mid \nlevel lapse rates around 7.5 C/km, will lead to around 2000 J/kg\nof MLCAPE. Capping will be weakest across western and northern \nMN which is where storms are expected to be most numerous \n(especially with northern extent where height falls will be more\nimpressive) late this afternoon and early evening. Bulk shear \naround 40 kts would allow for supercell development. Large hail \nand damaging winds are the primary threats, although the \nstrengthened LLJ will increase SRH to the point where a few \ntornadoes are also possible. Storm development south of I-94 \nappears improbable with the exception across far western MN due \nto greater capping and weaker forcing. However, maintained 20 \nPoPs in these areas for the potential that some of the initial \nactivity is maintained into the late evening hours. The \nincreasing LLJ throughout the day will also lead to gusty\nsoutherly winds as mixing depth maximizes. Some gusts exceeding\n40 mph are likely across central and southern MN.\n\nZonal flow will follow for Saturday, and by Sunday, a short \nwave will rotate southeast across MN into WI. This may bring a\nchance of showers across southern MN into IA and SD, but dry\nhigh pressure to the north will limit the northern extent of any\nprecip. The high will slide east to the Great Lakes Sunday night\nand warm air advection will begin across the Upper Midwest as a\npotent trough advances into the northern Plains. Very strong \nmoisture advection will be brought on by a 50-55 kt LLJ Monday \nand pwats are expected to increase to around 2 inches. \nIsentropic lift will allow widespread showers to develop across \nthe region, but the trough will swing through quickly and limit \nthe residence time of the activity. In addition, the surface\nwarm front will remain well to the south across southwestern IA\nand keep the more efficient thunderstorms anchored there. Models\nhave trended down on QPF locally from the 2-3 inches progged a\ncouple days ago. Given the lift and moisture in place, still\nthinking an inch is not unreasonable on a widespread basis with\nheavier amounts south and lighter amounts north, but this is\nshaping up to be another disappointing event in the fight \nagainst the drought.\n\nFor the rest of next week, zonal flow will continue with mid\nlevel ridging remaining anchored in place across the south. A \nnearly stationary front will draped from the central Plains to \nmid Mississippi Valley and will keep the hot and humid air well \nto the south. It will also act as a focus for numerous rounds of\nshowers and thunderstorms. At this time the odds of being under\nthe influence of dry high pressure outweigh the prospects of \nany significant rain near the front. Following the cold front \ntonight, we'll remain in a definitively cooler pattern. This not\nto say we won't hit 80 again at some point into early/mid \nOctober, but this front appears to mark the beginning of fall \nweather for the Upper Midwest.",
